Title: THE CALL OF THE WILD
Credit: Screenplay by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER ESTATE - GARDEN - NIGHT

Silver moonlight washes over the expansive manicured lawns and gravel paths of a Santa Clara Valley estate. BUCK, a magnificent 140-pound St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd cross with a thick dark coat and regal posture, walks quietly beside MANUEL, a slender gardener's helper in worn denim.

(SOUND: Night crickets, gentle breeze in tall poplars, crunching gravel)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing for every heavy, warm-coated dog from Puget Sound to San Diego. For men, groping in the Arctic darkness, had found a yellow metal.

Manuel leads Buck through the shadows of an orchard to a secluded flag station. STRANGER (40s, rough wool coat, heavy boots) steps out from the darkness.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel loops a piece of stout rope around Buck's neck beneath his collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Money chinks between them as coins pass from the Stranger to Manuel.

(SOUND: Coins chinking sharply, rope tightening)

Buck accepts the rope with quiet dignity, trusting men he knows. But as the ends are placed in the Stranger's hand, Buck growls menacingly.

The Stranger jerks the rope tight, shutting off Buck's breath. Buck springs in quick rage, but the Stranger grapples his throat, throwing him onto his back. Buck struggles furiously, his tongue lolling out, until his eyes glaze and he goes limp.

(SOUND: Harsh choking rasp, heavy body falling onto dirt)

INT. SALOON BACK SHED - SAN FRANCISCO - NIGHT

Sickly light from a single tallow candle illuminates a cramped, oil-stained shed filled with barrels. Buck lies on the wooden floor, throat raw and swollen, nursing his wounded pride.

(SOUND: Rain drumming on tin roof, distant harbor foghorn)

SALOON-KEEPER (50s, bulging cheeks) talks with the Stranger, who wraps a bloody handkerchief around a severely bitten hand.

STRANGER
All I get is fifty for it, an' I wouldn't do it over for a thousand, cold cash.

SALOON-KEEPER
That makes a hundred and fifty, and he's worth it, or I'm a squarehead. Lend me a hand before you pull your freight.

They throw Buck down, choking him repeatedly until they file the heavy brass collar from his neck and fling him into a narrow wooden crate.

(SOUND: Metal file rasping on brass, heavy crate door slamming)

INT. EXPRESS CAR - DAY

Dust flies through the wooden slats of a clattering train baggage car. Inside the narrow crate, Buck lies parched and starving, his eyes bloodshot, metamorphosed into a raging fiend.

(SOUND: Iron train wheels clattering on rails, steam locomotive whistle screeching)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For two days and nights in this express car, Buck neither ate nor drank. In his feverish anger he flapped against the bars, accumulating a fund of wrath that boded ill for whoever first fell foul of him.

EXT. SEATTLE BACKYARD - DAY

Rain-slicked wooden walls enclose a high-walled backyard. MAN IN RED SWEATER (burly, saggy sweater, grim smile) brought a hatchet and a heavy wooden club.

(SOUND: Heavy rain, hatchet splitting crate wood)

Four wagon drivers perch safely on top of the wall. The Man in Red Sweater drives his hatchet into the crate for a pry. Buck rushes at the splintering wood, teeth sinking into it.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

The man drops the hatchet and shifts the club to his right hand. Buck launches his 140 pounds of fury straight through the opening. In mid-air, the heavy club smashes down on his head.

(SOUND: Heavy club striking bone and flesh, violent snarling scream)

Buck crashes to the ground. He rises and launches himself again. Twelve times he charges, and every time the club smashes him down. Bleeding from nose and mouth, Buck staggered limply, beaten for the first time in his life.

The Man in Red Sweater fearlessly pats the head he has pounded, then offers a pan of water.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Answers to the name of Buck. Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is to let it go at that. You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

Buck drinks eagerly. PERRAULT (a short, dark French-Canadian courier in fur cap) enters the yard.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that. And seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

Money passes. Perrault leads Buck and CURLY (a friendly Newfoundland) away.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Freezing grey surf crashes against an icy Alaskan beach crowded with tents, gear, and gold-seekers. Buck steps out onto the cold snow, his paws sinking into white slush.

(SOUND: Icy ocean surf, howling Arctic wind, distant shouting)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck's first day on the Dyea beach was like a nightmare. He had been suddenly jerked from the heart of civilization and flung into the heart of things primordial. Here was neither peace, nor rest, nor a moment's safety.

Curly makes friendly advances to a wolfish husky. Without warning, the husky leaps in, ripping Curly's face open from eye to jaw.

A pack of forty huskies instantly surrounds them in a silent circle, then closes in on Curly.

(SOUND: Savage snarling chorus, heavy whip cracks, sickening tearing)

SPITZ (a large snow-white husky with a treacherous smile) stands nearby, running out his scarlet tongue in laughter.

FRANCOIS (a swarthy half-breed driver) and Perrault rush in with clubs and axes, scattering the pack, but Curly lies lifeless in the trampled bloody snow.

FRANCOIS
Sacredam!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Spitz ran out his tongue and laughed again, and from that moment Buck hated him with a bitter and deathless hatred.

Francois fastens leather harness straps onto Buck, placing him between DAVE (a gloomy, experienced wheeler) and lead-dog Spitz.

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. TRAIL - LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Pitch-black night encases a frozen lake beneath a 300-foot rock cliff. Driving snow whirls around a tiny driftwood fire.

(SOUND: Sub-zero gale howling, ice cracking beneath fire)

Buck digs a snug nest in the snow under the sheltering rock. He leaves to receive his fish ration from Francois, but returns to find Spitz occupying his nest.

Buck leaps upon Spitz with sudden roaring fury. They roll out in a wild tangle.

FRANCOIS
Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

Suddenly, eighty starving wild huskies from an Indian camp burst into the clearing, crazed by the scent of bacon.

(SOUND: Wild starving shrieks, clubs slamming into gaunt ribs, chaotic fight)

The famished invaders attack the team-dogs against the cliff. Buck is bitten on head and shoulders before the team breaks free and flees across the dark ice.

EXT. PELLY RIVER TRAIL - DAY

A narrow snow trail winds along a frozen, jagged riverbank beneath heavy pine branches.

(SOUND: Sled runners creaking on packed snow, rhythmic dog panting)

DOLLY (a team husky) lets out a heartbreaking wolf howl, mouth foaming madly, and springs straight for Buck.

Buck flees in terror across rough ice channels with mad Dolly panting one leap behind. He doubles back toward Francois.

Francois stands waiting with his axe poised. As Buck shoots past, the axe crashes down onto Dolly's head.

(SOUND: Heavy axe impact, sudden breath gasp)

Exhausted Buck collapses beside the sled. Spitz instantly leaps upon him, sinking teeth twice into Buck's unresisting neck.

Francois's lash descends violently upon Spitz, whipping him mercilessly.

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. All de tam I watch dat Buck I know for sure. Some dam fine day heem get mad lak hell an' den heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow. Sure. I know.

EXT. TAHKEENA CREEK - NIGHT

Pale cold moonlight floods a frozen creek bed winding through silent woods. A snowshoe rabbit flashes across the snow like a frost wraith.

(SOUND: Wind in pine tops, sixty dogs baying in full cry)

Buck leads a pack of sixty dogs in full cry after the rabbit, thrilled by the ancient blood-lust.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
All that stirring of old instincts which at stated periods drives men out to kill, the blood lust, the joy to kill--all this was Buck's. He was leading the pack, sounding the old wolf-cry, going back into the womb of Time.

Spitz cuts across a narrow neck of land and leaps from an overhanging bank, snapping the rabbit's back in mid-air.

The rabbit shrieks. Buck drives straight into Spitz shoulder-to-shoulder, missed the throat, and both roll over in the powdery snow.

The pack of sixty ill-tamed wolves forms a silent, waiting circle on the moonlit snow.

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder repeatedly. Buck is winded and streaming blood, but his imagination takes over. He feints a high shoulder rush, then sweeps low to the snow.

His teeth close on Spitz's left fore leg with a crunch of breaking bone. Buck repeats the trick, breaking Spitz's right fore leg.

(SOUND: Bone snap, vicious snarling)

Buck delivers a final shoulder charge. Spitz disappears beneath the dark, closing circle of huskies. Buck stands watching, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill.

EXT. CANADIAN TRAIL - DAY

Bright morning sun glints off fifty-below-zero snowdrifts along the Yukon trail.

(SOUND: Leather harness jangling, whip snapping)

Francois attempts to put Sol-leks at the lead position. Buck furiously drives Sol-leks back and stands in the leader's place.

FRANCOIS
Eh? Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job.

Francois brings a heavy club. Buck retreats slowly, keeping just beyond reach, snarling in open revolt. Perrault commands Francois to drop the club.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots in, laughing triumphantly, and swings into position at the head of the team.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! No, nevaire! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar!

Time shifts: weeks of heavy mail hauling pass. In late winter, DAVE (wheeler) grows mortally ill inside, crying out in pain.

The SCOTCH HALF-BREED (new mail driver) unhitches Dave to run free behind. Sick unto death, Dave resents being cut out of the traces, biting through replacement lines to stay in his place.

At the next camp stop, Dave cannot stand. The mail train pulls out, leaving Dave gasping in the snow.

(SOUND: Distant single revolver shot echoing through freezing woods)

Buck and the team listen in silence as the sleds churn on.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Muddy slush covers the busy wooden-plank street of Skaguay. Buck—now gaunt at 115 pounds, his coat matted and dull—stands dead weary in harness.

(SOUND: Crowded gold-rush town noise, shouting, sled bells)

HAL (19, callow, wearing a belt bristling with revolver and hunting knife) and CHARLES (middle-aged, watery eyes, drooping lip) pay gold to the mail agent.

MERCEDES (spoiled, wrapped in heavy furs) flutters around an enormous, top-heavy mountain of luggage on a single sled.

HAL
Mush! Mush on there!

Hal whips the exhausted dogs. The frozen sled runners hold fast.

TOWNSMAN
The runners are froze fast. Throw your weight against the gee-pole and break it out!

Hal sways the gee-pole. The overloaded sled surges forward, swings on a turn, and capsizes, scattering luggage across the street as Buck and team run wild.

(SOUND: Wooden sled crashing over, Mercedes screaming)

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

Blazing spring sun melts rotting river ice into dark fissures. At the mouth of White River, JOHN THORNTON (40s, rugged, weathered face, quiet eyes, wearing heavy flannel and leather) sits on a log whittling a birch axe-handle.

(SOUND: Trickling spring meltwater, river ice groaning under stress)

Buck collapses in his traces, a perambulating skeleton with loose skin draped over protruding ribs. Hal, Charles, and Mercedes pull up with their battered sled.

JOHN THORNTON
They told you true up above. The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. Only fools, with the blind luck of fools, could have made it. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
That's because you're not a fool, I suppose. All the same, we'll go on to Dawson. Get up there, Buck! Hi! Get up there! Mush on!

Hal trades his whip for a heavy wooden club and rains brutal blows onto Buck's head and body. Buck lies motionless, numb to pain, sensing impending doom on the rotten ice ahead.

John Thornton springs up with an animal cry, hurling Hal backward to the ground.

(SOUND: Body thud, Mercedes screaming)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. Get out of my way, or I'll fix you. I'm going to Dawson.

Hal draws his long hunting-knife. Thornton raps Hal's knuckles with the birch axe-handle, dropping the knife, then stoops and cuts Buck's traces.

Hal curses, forcing the remaining four dogs onto the rotten ice. A quarter-mile out, the rear of the sled drops through. Mercedes screams as the entire section of ice gives way, swallowing humans, dogs, and sled into the dark river.

(SOUND: Crackling ice collapse, distant screams, rushing water)

Thornton kneels beside Buck, rough hands searching for broken bones.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks Thornton's hand.

EXT. THORNTON CAMP - RIVERBANK - DAY

Lush green grass and blooming wildflowers cover a sunlit bank beside a clear mountain river. Buck, his muscle restored, coat gleaming silk-black, lies at the feet of John Thornton.

(SOUND: Gentle river current, wild bird song)

SKEET (a little Irish setter) gently washes Buck's healing wounds.

Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his hands, resting his own head upon Buck's, shaking him back and forth.

JOHN THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck playfully seizes Thornton's hand in his jaws, pressing his teeth in a feigned bite.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time. It had taken John Thornton to arouse it. Buck was content to adore at a distance, watching the outlines of the man who had saved his life.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON - STREET - DAY

Sixty-below-zero frost hangs in a white mist over the snow-packed main street of Dawson outside the Eldorado Saloon. Hundreds of fur-clad miners bank around a heavy sled loaded with twenty 50-pound sacks of flour.

(SOUND: Murmuring crowd, harsh freezing wind)

MATTHEWSON (a Bonanza King) slams a 1,000-dollar sack of gold dust onto a barrel.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't start a thousand pounds and walk off with it for a hundred yards.

Thornton borrows a 1,000-dollar gold sack from JIM O'BRIEN. Thornton kneels in the snow beside Buck, taking his head in his hands, cheek to cheek.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck seizes Thornton's mittened hand between his jaws in answer. Thornton steps back.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, Buck. Gee!

Buck swings right, lunging to snap the frozen runner grip. Crackle!

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck swings left. Snap! The runners slip side-to-side.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

(SOUND: Loud ice cracking snap, roaring cheer rising from crowd)

Buck throws his 150 pounds forward, muscles writhing under silky fur, claws tearing parallel grooves in the hard snow. The 1,000-pound sled lurches ahead inch by inch, gaining momentum across the 100 yards!

The crowd explodes into a roar. Thornton falls to his knees beside Buck, weeping, hugging Buck's head back and forth.

EXT. LOST CABIN VALLEY - DAY

Deep in an uncharted wilderness valley, a clear stream glints over placer gold. JOHN THORNTON, HANS, and PETE shovel gravel into sluice boxes, piling moose-hide sacks of gold dust near a spruce-bough lodge.

(SOUND: Sluice box water sloshing, distant wolf howl)

Buck stands at the edge of the dark forest, ears cocked, nose quivering toward a long-drawn wolf howl.

He leaps into the shadowed forest, finding a long, lean TIMBER WOLF erect on its haunches.

Buck approaches in menacing friendliness. The wolf flees; Buck overtakes him. They sniff noses, play coily, and run side-by-side through the twilight.

At a running stream, Buck stops, remembers Thornton, and turns back. The wild brother howls mournfully as Buck returns to camp.

EXT. MOOSE DIVIDE - NIGHT

Autumn frost glimmers on tall pines under a dark northern sky. A great bull moose—standing six feet high with 14-point antlers—stands enraged, a feathered arrow protruding from his flank.

(SOUND: Deep guttural moose roar, heavy hooves trampling brush)

Buck cuts the wounded bull out from the herd of twenty moose. For four days and nights, Buck harries the bull, never letting him eat, drink, or rest.

On the fourth night, the exhausted bull sways. Buck leaps, dragging the great moose down to the earth.

(SOUND: Heavy thud of massive body falling, violent thrashing)

EXT. DESOLATED CAMP - DAY

Smoke rises from the burned ruins of the spruce-bough lodge. NIG (the black hound) lies dead on the trail with an arrow through his body. HANS lies face down, feathered with arrows.

(SOUND: Sing-song Yeehat chanting, crackling embers)

A dozen YEEHAT HUNTERS dance around the wreckage.

Buck emerges from the brush—a live hurricane of fury, mane standing straight on end, eyes glowing with unholy rage.

(SOUND: Terrifying roaring howl, sudden screams, flesh tearing)

Buck leaps, ripping Chief Yeehat's throat wide open in a fountain of blood, then tears through two more hunters before they can raise spears.

YEEHAT HUNTER
The Evil Spirit! The Ghost Dog!

Panicked Yeehats flee in terror into the forest.

Buck walks slowly to the edge of a deep pool. Skeet lies dead at the water's edge. Thornton's scent leads straight into the deep water—with no trace leading away.

Buck sits by the pool, letting out a low, heartbreaking moan.

EXT. YEEHAT CAMP CLEARING - NIGHT

A full moon floods a white clearing with spectral light. Buck stands motionless as a statue in the center of the clearing.

(SOUND: Full wolf pack chorus howling, wind whistling)

A silvery flood of timber wolves pours out of the forest into the clearing.

The boldest wolf leaps at Buck; Buck strikes like lightning, breaking its neck. Three more charge; Buck slashes their throats, forcing the pack back against a gravel bank.

The wild brother timber wolf steps forward cautiously, whining softly. Buck touches noses with his wild brother.

An old gaunt wolf points his nose to the moon and howls. Buck points his nose to the sky and joins in the long wolf howl.

(SOUND: Triumphant wolf howl chorus echoing across mountains)

The pack leaps away into the forest. Buck bounds alongside his wild brother at the head of the pack.

EXT. LOST CABIN VALLEY - NIGHT

Cold aurora borealis flames in curtains of green and violet over the snow-covered valley.

(SOUND: Distant baying of the pack, icy wind)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the summers, a great gloriously coated wolf comes down into an open space among the trees where a yellow stream flows. He muses for a time, howling once, long and mournfully, ere he departs.

On top of the creek bank, the great Ghost Dog stands in the pale moonlight, howls once long and mournfully, then leaps into the forest at the head of the pack.

(SOUND: Final long mournful wolf howl fading into silence)
